{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"NOTE ON A LOCAL EXIT-1 THAT IS NOT A CODE DEFECT — recorded because \"7542 passed, exit 1\" would otherwise look like a hidden failure.\n\nThe final local full-suite run reported: 7542 passed, 29 skipped, 115 deselected, 0 failed, 0 errors — and exit code 1.\n\nCause: tests/conftest.py's pytest_sessionfinish REPO-ISOLATION guard. It snapshots `git status --porcelain` at session start, compares at finish, and escalates a clean run to TESTS_FAILED if the working tree gained entries — deliberately without emitting a FAILED line, so CI catches a test that wrote into the checkout instead of tmp_path.\n\nIt fired on MY contamination, not on the code: while that suite was running I edited infra/terraform/iam.tf and ran terraform init/apply in the SAME worktree for story 9249. `infra/terraform/iam.tf` was therefore newly-modified between snapshot and finish. (.terraform/ itself is gitignored, so the sole leaked entry was the .tf edit.)\n\nWhy this does not weaken this ticket's \"full gating suite passes\" criterion: that criterion is discharged by GERRIT CHANGE 1043's **Verified +1**, which is CI running the same gates on a FRESH checkout with no concurrent mutation — strictly better evidence than a local run. The local run's test result (7542 passed / 0 failed) is itself unaffected; only the session exit code was escalated.\n\nLESSON, and it is mine: do not mutate the working tree while a gating suite is running in it. The guard is correct and I tripped it. Future infra work in this epic should happen in a separate checkout, or be sequenced after the suite completes."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785430395157107001,"uuid":"8dc734f3-014e-49cc-a9e7-4bb6ad17239c"}